pages.push({slug:'engagements',title:'Work with TheFocus.AI',description:'A consulting and delivery partner for putting AI to work in your organization. Engagements from $20K per month.',active:'engagements/',html:hero('Work with us','Start with the change you want to make.','A team to think it through with you, build what’s needed, and help make the result useful in your business.')+`
<section class="section shell tight"><div class="offer"><div><span class="eyebrow signal">Consulting & implementation</span><h2>From the question<br />to the work.</h2><p class="lead">Some clients know exactly what they want to build. Others need to understand where AI can change their business. We can help at either starting point.</p><p>We bring business context and technical judgment into the same conversation, then assemble the team around the work.</p>${button('Talk with us')}</div><div class="engagement-note"><span class="eyebrow">Engagements from</span><div class="price">$20K <small>/ month</small></div><p>We agree the initial scope, team capacity, and commitment together. A focused project can be the beginning of a longer relationship; larger programs have a team and budget matched to their needs.</p><p class="micro">Pricing is a starting point, not a fixed package for every organization.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section shell"><div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">Different starting points</span><h2>Where are you<br />in the conversation?</h2></div><p class="lead">You don’t need to arrive with a finished specification. We can work from an ambition, a business problem, or an existing initiative.</p></div><div class="grid-3"><article class="card"><h3>Find a direction.</h3><p>Understand what the new capabilities mean for your organization and where they might create value.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Build something valuable.</h3><p>Bring a concrete idea, a constraint, or a system that needs to change. We help shape and deliver the work.</p></article><article class="card"><h3>Make a broader change.</h3><p>Connect related initiatives across systems and teams, with the continuity to put them into everyday use.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="section shell"><div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">Working together</span><h2>Clear responsibility.<br />Room to learn.</h2></div><p class="lead">The right engagement gives you something concrete to judge while leaving room for what the work reveals.</p></div><div class="grid-2"><article><h3>A team around your business.</h3><p>A delivery lead brings together the relevant engineering, data, and organizational expertise. We agree how we work with your people and where decisions sit.</p></article><article><h3>Progress you can see.</h3><p>Working systems, useful findings, and explicit decisions give you a basis for choosing what happens next. We agree how to assess the result in your context.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="section shell"><span class="eyebrow">Practical questions</span><h2>Before we begin.</h2>
${faq('Do we need a specific project in mind?','No. You may have a clear build, a problem that needs investigation, or a wider question about how AI changes your organization. The first conversation helps establish a useful way to work together.')}
${faq('What does the monthly starting price cover?','An agreed allocation of delivery capacity around a defined scope. Team composition and commitment depend on the work. We distinguish delivery fees from infrastructure, model usage, and third-party software costs in the proposal.')}
${faq('Is every engagement a two-month project?','No. Around two months and $40K can be a useful starting shape for a focused build, but it is an example rather than a required sequence. We agree the scope, timing, and initial commitment for your situation.')}
${faq('Can you work with our existing team and systems?','Yes. We can extend an existing team, build alongside it, or take responsibility for a defined area. Decisions about preserving or replacing systems follow the business need.')}
${faq('What happens as the work grows?','We agree the next scope and the capacity it needs. An engagement can expand into a broader program or ongoing development and operation, with responsibilities and commercial terms made explicit.')}
${faq('Who owns what we build?','Project code, configuration, documentation, and client resources should have a clear home in your organization. We agree ownership and access in the scope, including any third-party licensing considerations.')}
</section>${cta()}`});

pages.push({slug:'approach',title:'The Org Age of AI — our approach',description:'How knowledge, software, and ways of working come together to turn AI capability into organizational value.',active:'approach/',html:hero('Our approach','The organization is where the value is made.','AI expands the capabilities available to a business. Realizing their value depends on how knowledge, systems, and people come together.')+`
<section class="section shell tight"><div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">The premise</span><h2>Make new capability<br />part of the business.</h2></div><div><p class="lead">Useful intelligence needs context. Useful software needs a place in the work. New capacity needs somewhere valuable to go.</p><p>Those connections are organizational as well as technical. We work across them, from understanding the opportunity to building and putting systems into use.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section shell"><span class="eyebrow">Three connected capabilities</span><div class="grid-3"><article class="card"><span class="eyebrow">Intelligent data</span><h3>What does the organization know?</h3><p>Important knowledge lives in systems, documents, and experience. Making it available is only part of the work; the business context and meaning have to travel with it.</p><p>We connect information and build the foundations for useful answers and better decisions.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="eyebrow">Intelligent software</span><h3>What can the organization do?</h3><p>Software can work with richer context, carry out more complex tasks, and support ideas that were previously impractical.</p><p>We build systems and experiences around what that makes possible for your business.</p></article><article class="card"><span class="eyebrow">Intelligent organization</span><h3>How does the work come together?</h3><p>New capabilities change the possibilities for teams, responsibilities, and services. The value depends on how the organization puts them to use.</p><p>We help connect the systems we build to the people and decisions around them.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="section shell"><div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">Questions that shape the work</span><h2>A framework for<br />making choices.</h2></div><p class="lead">The Org Age of AI gives us a way to examine the connections. Your situation determines which ones need attention.</p></div><div class="grid-2"><article><h3>Is the knowledge usable?</h3><p>Can the business make its context available beyond the people who already know where to look and what things mean?</p></article><article><h3>What changes when software can do more?</h3><p>Which activities, services, or ways of coordinating work become possible? What should remain a choice people make?</p></article><article><h3>What makes the result trustworthy?</h3><p>What evidence does this particular business need before relying on a system? The answer depends on the consequences and the kind of work.</p></article><article><h3>Where will the value go?</h3><p>How does new capacity become growth, better service, useful insight, or something the organization could not do before?</p></article></div></section>
<section class="section shell"><div class="book-panel"><div><span class="eyebrow">Explore the argument</span><h2>The Org Age of AI</h2><p class="lead">The book connects these questions across organizational knowledge, enterprise change, workflows, verification, and value.</p><a class="text-link" href="${link('book/')}">Explore the book ↗</a></div><div><h3>Thinking informs the build.<br />The build informs the thinking.</h3><p>Our practical work gives us a way to test these ideas against real organizations. The cases show the specific choices and limits in each setting.</p><a class="text-link" href="${link('work/')}">See selected work ↗</a></div></div></section>${cta()}`});

pages.push({slug:'work',title:'Selected operational work',description:'Perplexity on Samsung TVs, investment research, construction finance, and enterprise media systems—with delivered work distinguished from future scope.',active:'work/',html:hero('Selected work','Organizational questions.<br />Real systems.','Examples of connecting what a business knows, what its software can do, and how its people put that capability to work.')+`<section class="section shell tight"><div class="grid-3">${caseCards()}</div><p class="micro" style="margin-top:25px">Each case distinguishes delivered work, active work, and proposed expansion. Business impact is stated only where it is supported.</p></section><section class="section shell"><div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">Different settings. Different choices.</span><h2>New capability needs a place in the business.</h2></div><p class="lead">These projects start in different places. Each connects technical work to the organization’s ability to understand, act, or grow.</p></div>${rows}</section>${cta('What does this suggest for your business?')}`});
for (const c of cases) pages.push({slug:'work/'+c.slug,title:c.name+' — '+c.claim,description:c.short,active:'work/',html:hero(c.name,c.title,c.short)+`<section class="section shell tight"><div class="article-layout"><aside><p><strong>Operation</strong>${c.facts[0]}</p><p><strong>Evidence</strong>${c.facts[1]}</p><p><strong>Next</strong>${c.facts[2]}</p><a class="text-link" href="${link('work/')}">← All selected work</a></aside><article class="prose"><span class="eyebrow">${c.status}</span><h2>The problem</h2><p>${c.problem}</p><div class="case-flow">${c.flow.map(([h,d],i)=>`<div><span class="eyebrow">0${i+1} / ${h}</span><p>${d}</p></div>`).join('')}</div><h2>The work</h2><p>${c.built}</p><h2>What the evidence supports</h2><div class="note"><p>${c.evidence}</p></div><h2>People and decisions</h2><p>${c.human}</p><h2>The next useful step</h2><p>${c.next}</p><h2>What to look for in your business</h2><p>The relevant starting point may be a constraint you already recognize or something new you want the business to be able to do. The case is one example of connecting that question to a practical change.</p><a class="text-link" href="${link('brief/')}">Write a brief for that operation ↗</a></article></div></section>${cta()}`});

pages.push({slug:'about',title:'A team for the Org Age of AI',description:'TheFocus.AI combines delivery leadership, engineering, data, verification, and adoption around valuable business operations.',html:hero('About TheFocus.AI','Thinking and building<br />for the Org Age of AI.','We help companies understand what new intelligence makes possible, and build the systems to put it to work.')+`<section class="section shell tight"><div class="grid-2"><div><h2>Responsibility stays clear. Expertise comes together.</h2><p class="lead">Each engagement has a named delivery lead and an agreed team. Engineering, data, verification, and operational knowledge work toward the same outcome.</p><p>We combine senior judgment with a team built around the engagement. The relationship can grow as the opportunity becomes clearer and the work develops.</p></div><div><img class="photo" src="/assets/studio-room-wide.jpg" alt="TheFocus.AI studio workspace" width="1200" height="600" loading="lazy"/><p class="photo-caption">A place for building, reviewing, and working through the details.</p></div></div></section><section class="section shell"><div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">What we take responsibility for</span><h2>More than a technical handoff.</h2></div><p class="lead">Understanding the opportunity, building the systems, and helping the organization put them to use are connected parts of the job.</p></div><div class="grid-3"><article><h3>Clarity before commitment.</h3><p>We define the first outcome, the scope, and the evidence needed to accept the work.</p></article><article><h3>Visible progress.</h3><p>You review working increments and make tradeoffs with the people responsible for delivery.</p></article><article><h3>Ownership after delivery.</h3><p>Operating knowledge, project materials, and system access have a clear home in your organization.</p></article></div></section><section class="section shell"><div class="grid-2"><div><span class="eyebrow">Leadership & thinking</span><h2>Built from practice.</h2><p>Will Schenk leads TheFocus.AI. His work with Ksenia Se of Turing Post on <em>The Org Age of AI</em> examines why technical capability does not automatically become organizational value.</p><p>That work informs the way the team scopes, builds, and verifies operational systems.</p><a class="text-link" href="${link('book/')}">Explore the book ↗</a></div><div><span class="eyebrow">Working with partners</span><h2>Extend your ability to deliver.</h2><p>We also work with partners bringing deep client relationships or domain expertise. Responsibilities, client communication, and delivery boundaries are agreed explicitly.</p><p>Our work on enterprise media systems is one example. Both the partner and end client remain confidential.</p><a class="text-link" href="${link('contact/?interest=partner')}">Discuss a delivery partnership ↗</a></div></div></section>${cta()}`});

pages.push({slug:'book',title:'Download The Org Age of AI',description:'Get the free book: seven chapters connecting organizational knowledge, workflows, verification, people, and business value. Read chapter excerpts before downloading.',active:'book/',html:`<section class="page-hero shell"><a class="breadcrumb" href="${link('')}">TheFocus.AI / The book</a><div class="book-hero"><div><span class="eyebrow">A field guide for business leaders</span><h1>The Org Age<br />of AI</h1><p class="lead">Why the return on AI is organizational—and what that means for the company you are building.</p><p>By Will Schenk &amp; Ksenia Se<br />TheFocus.AI × Turing Post</p><ul class="benefit-list"><li>Understand why good tools alone don’t change a company.</li><li>Make your organization’s knowledge useful.</li><li>Recognize where software can change the work.</li><li>Connect new capacity to business value.</li></ul><div class="reading-strip"><div><strong>7</strong>chapters + appendix</div><div><strong>133</strong>pages</div><div><strong>Free</strong>PDF edition</div></div><p class="micro">Current working edition. The next editorial revision is in development.</p></div><div id="download">${bookPromo('book-page')}</div></div></section>
<section class="section shell tight"><div class="section-head"><div><span class="eyebrow">Read before you download</span><h2>Start with a question<br />that matters to you.</h2></div><p class="lead">These are passages from the book’s source chapters, with room to read the argument rather than just a summary of it.</p></div><div class="grid-3">${chapterCards()}</div></section>
<section class="section shell"><div class="article-layout"><aside><span class="eyebrow">Inside the current edition</span><p>Seven connected arguments, from organizational readiness to the people who make change possible.</p><a class="text-link" href="#download">Download the book ↓</a></aside><div><ol class="book-toc"><li><div><h3>AI Feels Powerful. So Why Is the ROI Still Missing?</h3><p>Why the organization’s ability to absorb intelligence determines what it can gain from it.</p><a href="${link('book/why-roi-is-missing/')}">Read an excerpt ↗</a></div></li><li><div><h3>The Unsexy Truth of AI Adoption</h3><p>A framework for understanding organizational readiness, shared meaning, and usable knowledge.</p><a href="${link('book/making-the-organization-legible/')}">Read an excerpt ↗</a></div></li><li><div><h3>There Are No AI-Native Enterprises</h3><p>The authority, coordination, and existing ways of working that shape change inside established companies.</p></div></li><li><div><h3>AI Workflow Patterns</h3><p>How to recognize the work that can change, and the patterns that help you think about it.</p><a href="${link('book/choosing-where-to-begin/')}">Read an excerpt ↗</a></div></li><li><div><h3>The Flywheel: What Happens When Workflows Run Themselves</h3><p>Verification, feedback, and the conditions for systems to take on more responsibility.</p></div></li><li><div><h3>AI Saved Time. Where Did the Value Go?</h3><p>How released capacity becomes a business outcome—and where that connection breaks down.</p><a href="${link('book/value-of-an-hour/')}">Explore: what is an hour saved worth? ↗</a></div></li><li><div><h3>How to Hire for AI</h3><p>The roles and responsibilities involved in making organizational intelligence useful.</p></div></li></ol><p class="micro" style="margin-top:25px">Also includes: the startup appendix, frameworks, cases, a self-assessment, and glossary.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section shell"><div class="grid-2"><div><span class="eyebrow">More than a download</span><h2>Ideas you can keep<br />putting to work.</h2><p class="lead">TheFocus.AI’s email list continues the conversation with practical questions, field notes from building systems, and examples of what organizations are learning.</p><p>When a question turns into something you want to build, there is a team you can talk to.</p><a class="text-link" href="${link('contact/')}">Talk with TheFocus.AI ↗</a></div><div class="letter-card"><span class="eyebrow">What the book helps you ask</span><h3>What do we know that we can’t yet use?</h3><h3>What could we do that used to be impractical?</h3><h3>What needs to change for the value to reach the business?</h3></div></div></section>${cta('What would you like to put into practice?')}`});

for (const e of bookExcerpts) pages.push({slug:'book/'+e.slug,title:e.title+' — The Org Age of AI',description:e.description,active:'book/',html:hero('From the book / Chapter '+e.chapter,e.title,e.description)+`<section class="section shell tight"><div class="article-layout"><aside><p><strong>The Org Age of AI</strong>Will Schenk &amp; Ksenia Se</p><p><strong>Reading time</strong>${e.minutes} minutes</p><a class="text-link" href="${link('book/')}">See the complete book ↗</a></aside><article class="prose excerpt-prose">${e.html}<p class="source-note">Excerpt from the current book’s source chapter, “${e.heading}.” Original prose preserved. First published in <a href="${e.url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Turing Post</a>. The book is being revised into its next edition.</p><aside class="book-promo"><span class="eyebrow">Keep reading</span><h2>Get the whole argument.</h2><p>Download The Org Age of AI and join TheFocus.AI’s email list for practical ideas about putting it to work.</p>${bookCapture('excerpt-'+e.chapter)}</aside></article></div></section>${cta('What does this bring to mind in your business?')}`});
const bookDownloadUrl = import.meta.env.PUBLIC_BOOK_DOWNLOAD_URL || '';
pages.push({slug:'book/download',title:'Your copy of The Org Age of AI',description:'Download the current book edition and choose where to begin reading.',active:'book/',html:hero('Your reading copy','Your book is ready.','Start with the question that brought you here. Keep the book close as your thinking develops.')+`<section class="section shell tight"><div class="grid-2"><div class="download-card"><span class="eyebrow">TheFocus.AI × Turing Post</span><h2>The Org Age of AI</h2><p>Will Schenk &amp; Ksenia Se<br />Current working edition · 133 pages · PDF</p>${bookDownloadUrl ? `<a class="button" href="${bookDownloadUrl}" download="The-Org-Age-of-AI.pdf">Download the PDF <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>` : '<p>This review build does not include the downloadable PDF.</p>'}<p class="micro" style="margin-top:20px">Preview mode: no email was stored or sent, and you have not been added to a mailing list.</p></div><div><span class="eyebrow">Turn a useful idea into a conversation</span><h2>Have something<br />you want to build?</h2><p class="lead">TheFocus.AI helps organizations put these ideas into practice. Bring a question, a business ambition, or a project you want to move forward.</p>${button('Discuss a project')}<p class="micro" style="margin-top:20px">Consulting & delivery from $20K / month.</p></div></div></section><section class="section shell"><span class="eyebrow">A few places to begin</span><div class="grid-3">${chapterCards()}</div></section>`});

pages.push({slug:'brief',title:'Write your first-project brief',description:'A practical worksheet for defining the operation, outcome, owner, and acceptance criteria for your first AI implementation project.',html:hero('Your first-project brief','Make the first conversation useful.','If you have an idea for a project, use this optional worksheet to develop it. You can also start a conversation before the shape of the work is clear.')+`<section class="section shell tight"><div class="form-layout"><aside><h2>Start with what you know.</h2><p class="lead">Use the questions that help you think. Leave the others open. A brief can develop from a new opportunity as well as an existing problem.</p><ul class="check-list"><li>Choose one recurring operation.</li><li>Name the consequence of the current problem.</li><li>Identify who can decide and validate.</li><li>Write down what better would look like.</li></ul><p class="micro" style="margin-top:25px">Your answers stay in this page until you download them. They are not sent or stored.</p></aside><div class="form-panel"><form id="brief-form"><div class="field"><label for="operation">1. Which operation should work better?</label><textarea id="operation" name="operation" required placeholder="What would you like your team or organization to be able to do?"></textarea></div><div class="field"><label for="consequence">2. What does the current problem cost you?</label><textarea id="consequence" name="consequence" required placeholder="Time spent, delays, errors, risk, or work you cannot currently take on. Label estimates as estimates."></textarea></div><div class="form-row"><div class="field"><label for="owner">3. Who owns the decision?</label><input id="owner" name="owner" placeholder="Role or person"/></div><div class="field"><label for="systems">4. Where does the work live?</label><input id="systems" name="systems" placeholder="Systems, files, and teams"/></div></div><div class="field"><label for="acceptance">5. What would prove it works?</label><textarea id="acceptance" name="acceptance" required placeholder="A measurable result, representative cases, and the person who will accept the work."></textarea></div><div class="field"><label for="exceptions">6. What still needs a human decision?</label><textarea id="exceptions" name="exceptions" placeholder="Ambiguous items, approvals, or consequential actions."></textarea></div><div class="field"><label for="capacity">7. What will you do with the capacity released?</label><textarea id="capacity" name="capacity" placeholder="Handle more volume, reduce cycle time, improve quality, or create a new capability. Name an owner."></textarea></div><div class="form-row"><div class="field"><label for="timing">8. When would you want to begin?</label><select id="timing" name="timing"><option>Still exploring</option><option>Within 30 days</option><option>Within 90 days</option><option>Later this year</option></select></div><div class="field"><label for="investment">9. Initial investment range</label><select id="investment" name="investment"><option>Not yet agreed</option><option>Under $20K</option><option>$20K–$40K</option><option>$40K–$80K</option><option>$80K+</option></select></div></div><button class="button" type="submit">Build my brief <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></button></form><section class="result" id="brief-result" hidden aria-live="polite"><h3>Your first-project brief</h3><pre id="brief-output"></pre><div class="actions"><button class="button ghost" id="download-brief" type="button">Download brief ↓</button><a class="text-link" href="${link('contact/')}">Discuss the operation ↗</a></div><p class="micro">Download before leaving this page. Your answers are not saved automatically.</p></section></div></div></section>`});

pages.push({slug:'contact',title:'Talk about what’s next for your organization',description:'Discuss the workflow, business outcome, and a useful first implementation engagement with TheFocus.AI.',html:hero('Start a conversation','What are you thinking about?','Tell us what AI could mean for your business, what you want to build, or where you’re unsure how to begin.')+`<section class="section shell tight"><div class="form-layout"><aside><span class="eyebrow signal">A useful first conversation</span><h2>An ambition.<br />A question. A project.</h2><p>You don’t need a finished brief to start a conversation. We’ll explore what you’re trying to do, where we might help, and whether there is useful work to do together.</p><div class="note"><p><strong>Engagements start around $20K per month.</strong></p><p>We shape the work and the team around your situation, from a focused initial engagement to a broader program.</p></div><h3 style="margin-top:35px">Start wherever you are.</h3><p>A new idea, a persistent business problem, or a question about the direction of your organization can all be useful starting points.</p><p style="margin-top:25px">Still framing the problem? <a href="${link('brief/')}">Start with the project brief.</a></p></aside><div class="form-panel"><form data-preview-form="contact"><div class="form-row"><div class="field"><label for="contact-name">Your name</label><input id="contact-name" name="name" required autocomplete="name"/></div><div class="field"><label for="contact-email">Work email</label><input id="contact-email" name="email" type="email" required autocomplete="email"/></div></div><div class="field"><label for="company">Company</label><input id="company" name="company" required autocomplete="organization"/></div><div class="field"><label for="interest">What would you like to discuss?</label><select id="interest" name="interest"><option value="operation">Putting AI to work in our organization</option><option value="program">A larger delivery program</option><option value="partner">A delivery partnership</option></select></div><div class="field"><label for="challenge">What would you like to explore?</label><textarea id="challenge" name="challenge" required placeholder="What you want to make possible, a question you’re working through, or a project you have in mind."></textarea></div><div class="form-row"><div class="field"><label for="contact-timing">When would you like to start?</label><select id="contact-timing" name="timing"><option>Within 30 days</option><option>Within 90 days</option><option>Still exploring</option></select></div><div class="field"><label for="contact-budget">Initial investment range</label><select id="contact-budget" name="budget"><option>Not yet agreed</option><option>Under $20K</option><option>$20K–$40K</option><option>$40K–$80K</option><option>$80K+</option></select></div></div><button class="button" type="submit">Request a conversation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></button><p class="micro" style="margin-top:16px">Interactive preview. No enquiry is sent and no meeting is booked.</p></form><section class="result" data-form-result hidden aria-live="polite"></section></div></div></section>`});

pages.push({slug:'book/value-of-an-hour',title:'What is an hour saved actually worth?',description:'Connect AI capacity to growth, better decisions, and new services. A practical question from The Org Age of AI.',active:'book/',html:hero('A question from the book','What is an hour<br />saved actually worth?','The useful question is what your organization can do with the capacity it gains.')+`<section class="section shell tight"><div class="article-layout"><aside><p><strong>The Org Age of AI</strong>A companion to Chapter 6</p><p><strong>For business leaders</strong>Growth, decisions, and new capability</p><a class="text-link" href="${link('book/')}">Explore the book ↗</a></aside><article class="prose"><p class="lead">A team finishes its work sooner. Has the business become more valuable?</p><p>Perhaps. The team might take on another customer, investigate an issue it previously had to ignore, or develop an offering that never made economic sense. It might also finish sooner and wait for the same next meeting.</p><h2>The value depends on what changes.</h2><p>An hour of a salaried employee’s time has a cost, but recovering that hour does not automatically reduce payroll. The immediate gain is room to do something else. The business value depends on what becomes possible—and whether the organization makes use of it.</p><div class="table-wrap"><table><caption>Different uses of the same saved hour</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">What changes?</th><th scope="col">What would show value?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>A team can serve more customers.</td><td>Additional profitable work, with demand and delivery capacity to support it.</td></tr><tr><td>A decision happens sooner.</td><td>A specific opportunity gained or problem addressed because the timing changed.</td></tr><tr><td>Paid outside work is no longer needed.</td><td>Spending that actually falls, after the costs of the new system.</td></tr><tr><td>The team can investigate more deeply.</td><td>A change in quality, service, or the problems it catches.</td></tr><tr><td>A new service becomes practical.</td><td>Customers can receive something the business could not previously afford to offer.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h2>Look beyond the stopwatch.</h2><p>A small improvement where work is held up may matter more than a large saving elsewhere. A faster response matters when it reaches a customer at the right moment. A faster analysis matters when someone can act on it.</p><p>Measure the whole effort, including checking and correction. Then ask what the business did differently. Counting the same benefit once as saved wages and again as new output can make an attractive estimate that the business never receives.</p><h2>Sometimes the value is a new capability.</h2><p>Perplexity’s Samsung TV app illustrates a different starting point: bringing AI into a customer experience. There is no need to invent an old manual process or an hourly saving to explain why that product work matters.</p><a class="text-link" href="${link('work/perplexity-samsung/')}">Explore the Perplexity TV project ↗</a><p style="margin-top:28px">Other work starts with knowledge the organization already has. At Steering House, that means making financial patterns useful to project managers. At Trinity Hunt, it means bringing research criteria and deal information into tools the investment team can use.</p><div class="actions"><a class="text-link" href="${link('work/steering-house/')}">Steering House ↗</a><a class="text-link" href="${link('work/trinity-hunt/')}">Trinity Hunt ↗</a></div><h2>What would your business do with more capacity?</h2><p>Start with an opportunity you care about. What would become possible? What is holding it back? What would need to change around the technology for the business to benefit?</p><p>Those questions shape the work we do at TheFocus.AI: identify a valuable opportunity, build the capability, and help the organization put it to use.</p>${button('Discuss the opportunity')}<p class="micro" style="margin-top:30px">An original website companion to Chapter 6, “AI Saved Time. Where Did the Value Go?” The project examples distinguish delivered capabilities from outcomes still to be measured.</p><aside class="book-promo"><span class="eyebrow">Continue with the book</span><h2>From new intelligence<br />to a more capable organization.</h2><p>Download The Org Age of AI and join our email list for practical ideas about putting it to work.</p>${bookCapture('value-of-hour')}</aside></article></div></section>${cta('What could your organization become capable of?')}`});
